{"success": true, "data": {"stdout": "\section{Discussion and Conclusion}
\label{sec:discussion}

We formalized recursive generation depth estimation and showed that perplexity tail statistics achieve 70--85\% four-class accuracy ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.75}{=}4$), with five features sufficing for 97.6\% of full performance and classifier-agnostic convergence within $\leq$0.4pp (\S\ref{sec:atlas:edd}).
The dominant features (\texttt{p90\_ppl} top-2 in 9/9 cells, \texttt{var\_surprisal} in 8/9) generalize across all three model families without retraining, making depth estimation a candidate early-warning signal for model collapse~\cite{shumailov2024_nature,shi2025_closer_look}; surface-text ablation confirms the signal is inaccessible from text alone (zero-shot LLMs at 30--36\%, 42\,pp below RF; \S\ref{sec:gen:baselines}).
Cross-strategy transfer remains the primary bottleneck (59.1\% vs.\ 66.4\% cross-model), requiring a two-stage pipeline (strategy identification + conditioned classification) for deployment.
Depth estimation enables depth-graduated curation (retaining d1, filtering d3+), complementing binary detection~\cite{dsir2023,qurating2024} and watermarking~\cite{kirchenbauer2023_watermark} as a provenance signal~\cite{bevendorff2025_two_paradigms}.
